WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.720
Carl Sagan, he has written&nbsp;
so many popular science books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.360
and brought us the original Cosmos series which&nbsp;
has inspired so many people to dream about space.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.920
The surface of the earth is&nbsp;
the shore of the cosmic ocean.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.360
On this shore we've learned most of what we know.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.240
Recently we've waded a little way out, maybe&nbsp;
ankle deep, and the water seems inviting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:35.920
Some part of our being knows this is where we&nbsp;
came from. We long to return and we can because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:44.240
the cosmos is also within us, we're made of star&nbsp;
stuff. We are a way for the cosmos to know itself.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:51.200
Sagan was also an accomplished researcher and is&nbsp;
one of the most highly cited planetary scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:58.240
so I wanted to take a look today at where that&nbsp;
all started by taking a look at his PhD thesis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:05.360
Now Sagan went to the University of&nbsp;
Chicago and he completed his PhD in 1960&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:12.240
when he was age 25. The title is 'Physical&nbsp;
studies of planets' and his supervisor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.160
was Gerard Kuiper of whom the&nbsp;
Kuiper belt is named after.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.880
On the cover page here we&nbsp;
can see the name Carl Sagan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.920
and his signature. The thesis is in four parts.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.520
Part one: Indigenous organic matter on the moon.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.000
Part two: biological contamination of the moon.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.840
Part three: the radiation balance of Venus.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.760
Part 4: production of organic&nbsp;
molecules and planetary atmospheres.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:51.840
These topics reflect his interest in astrobiology,&nbsp;
he was known for thinking a lot about life in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.160
the cosmos and he was a big promoter of SETI -&nbsp;
the search for extraterrestrial intelligence.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.840
There is some chance that in the next few decades&nbsp;
we will get a signal from some spectacularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:12.480
distant spectacularly exotic civilization and&nbsp;
everything on earth will as a consequence change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.040
If there's a plausible argument&nbsp;
that there isn't anybody out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.800
bearing in mind that we can be wrong we ought&nbsp;
to keep looking because the question is of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:28.400
most supreme importance. It calibrates our place&nbsp;
in the universe, it tells us who we are and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.840
it is worthwhile trying to find other&nbsp;
civilizations I would say no matter what.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.080
Part one starts off by saying that&nbsp;
for some time after its formation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:46.640
the moon possessed an atmosphere composed of&nbsp;
methane, ammonia, water vapor and hydrogen, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:54.800
says the effect of solar UV light on an atmosphere&nbsp;
is that organic molecules of fair complexity are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:03:02.160
produced and the purpose of this present paper is&nbsp;
to estimate the quantity of organic matter which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:08.880
formed in the primitive lunar atmosphere, diffused&nbsp;
to the surface and survived to the present day.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.160
This was written 10 years before the&nbsp;
Apollo astronauts would land on the moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:21.120
and Sagan is pretty interested in the idea of&nbsp;
finding organic molecules there. Organic molecules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:29.120
I guess are of special interest since they are&nbsp;
precursors to life or are used a lot in biology.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:35.520
You may have heard that the moon has such a weak&nbsp;
atmosphere now that it could be considered to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:42.240
no atmosphere or actually be considered a pretty&nbsp;
good vacuum but Sagan is referencing material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.720
that says that that wasn't always the case. The&nbsp;
moon's atmosphere requires replenishment from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:56.320
interior of the moon and gradually escapes so the&nbsp;
moon's atmosphere might once have been very thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:04.080
before most of it has been lost to space. Sagan&nbsp;
first works out a surface density of synthesized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:10.800
material so this would be a covering of these&nbsp;
organic molecules and it's more common to hear of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.200
uv light breaking down molecules than helping to&nbsp;
build them but remember that sunlight is thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:24.240
to be an essential part of the equation for how&nbsp;
we got life in the first place. That the effect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:31.280
UV radiation on bonds and molecules helps them&nbsp;
break down but also builds into longer chains.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.680
The effect of heat and UV light on the molecules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:42.720
is remarkable, he says. A large fraction of&nbsp;
the molecules partake in organic synthesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:50.160
of a higher order of complexity. Polypeptides&nbsp;
arise from amino acids, hydrocarbon dimers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:57.120
form long chain polymers and in general very&nbsp;
complex organic molecules are constructed.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.840
This continued radiation and high temperatures&nbsp;
would eventually destroy these molecules but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:09.200
during this time there were a lot of meteors&nbsp;
hitting the moon and a lot of this meteorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:16.560
material falling into the lunar atmosphere so&nbsp;
Sagan says the moon's surface must have received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:24.080
a dust cover probably composed primarily of&nbsp;
silicates and ices. The organic molecules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.320
would then be covered by a protective layer&nbsp;
insulating them from the extremes of temperature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:38.880
and absorbing the radiation so he says that&nbsp;
provided no large-scale destructive events we may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:45.680
anticipate the presence of complex organic matter&nbsp;
beneath the moon's dust layer. This would only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:51.760
apply to regions that have had no extensive lava&nbsp;
flows, the southern highland appears to be such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:59.120
a region as does much of the far side of the moon&nbsp;
and he finishes this first section of the thesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:06.400
by saying that to figure out much more what we'll&nbsp;
need is a sample of lunar material from below the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:13.040
surface. Scientists have since found some of these&nbsp;
molecules in samples that we took from the moon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:19.840
and although we haven't found any life there we&nbsp;
have found some of the essential ingredients.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:28.240
Part two is about the biological contamination of&nbsp;
the moon. Sagan is expecting that soon humans will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.840
start landing on the moon and he expresses&nbsp;
concern that terrestrial microorganisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:41.440
meaning from earth and organic matter deposited&nbsp;
with the packages may obscure detection of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.840
or interact with possible organisms or&nbsp;
organic matter indigenous to the moon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:55.680
Such biological contamination of the moon would&nbsp;
represent an unparalleled scientific disaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:02.800
and because of it, scientific opportunities lost&nbsp;
on the moon may not be recoupable elsewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:10.880
He lists four classes of possible lunar biological&nbsp;
contamination and I haven't found reference to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.840
these words anywhere outside this thesis so&nbsp;
maybe they are words of his own invention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:24.240
but the first one is biomixy which is that the&nbsp;
moon may contain no indigenous living organisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:31.040
and may be incapable of supporting terrestrial&nbsp;
organisms. However there may be relics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:38.080
primitive indigenous organisms deposited on or&nbsp;
beneath the surface and a vehicle impacting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:45.760
moon may distribute its contents over the surface&nbsp;
such that further investigations might be unable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:52.560
to distinguish between the primitive indigenous&nbsp;
organisms and ones that came along from earth.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:59.280
This is pretty similar to the second type listed&nbsp;
as sapromixy and then we have the third type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:07.120
phagomixy which is that the moon may contain no&nbsp;
indigenous living organisms but may be capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:13.520
of supporting some terrestrial organisms.&nbsp;
This would require subsurface organic matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:20.960
moisture and heat sources. The possibility then&nbsp;
exists that deposited microorganisms from earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:29.440
in the absence of any competitors or predators&nbsp;
will multiply at a geometric rate limited only by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:36.640
the availability of water and metabolites. This&nbsp;
would give a biological explosion and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:42.080
probably see that this wouldn't be very helpful&nbsp;
for trying to study the history of the moon.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:49.680
And the fourth type of contamination is&nbsp;
ecomixy, that the moon itself may contain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:56.000
indigenous living organisms, that is that there&nbsp;
may be life on the moon. There is then the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:03.040
possibility that deposited microorganisms from&nbsp;
earth by competition with or parasitism upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:10.320
even one species of lunar organism will disrupt&nbsp;
the ecology completely. So these are kind of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:17.120
the ways we could screw things up on the moon by&nbsp;
accidentally depositing some of our own biology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:24.960
and doing a bit of math and looking at a few of&nbsp;
the numbers including the survival law which would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.000
tell how long the organisms from earth could&nbsp;
even survive if they were accidentally left on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.960
the moon, Sagan comes up with a few conclusions&nbsp;
about how likely each of these possibilities are.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.720
He finds that most microorganisms would be&nbsp;
killed by the UV radiation within a few hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.880
but if any make their way into a lunar crevasse&nbsp;
or are otherwise shielded from the radiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:56.080
then they might survive for quite some time&nbsp;
and while the possibilities may be low of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:02.080
life spreading or surviving or even getting&nbsp;
there in the first place, Sagan finds that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:08.240
of those types of contamination cannot&nbsp;
be excluded and are not negligible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.720
and I suppose the whole paper is a little bit&nbsp;
of a warning to make sure that when spacecraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:19.760
leave earth on their way to the moon&nbsp;
that we sterilize them really thoroughly.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:27.920
The third part of the thesis the radiation balance&nbsp;
of Venus. It is the longest section and probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:35.840
contains the most notable work. Sagan was trying&nbsp;
to explain a recent microwave observation of Venus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:43.680
that gave its surface temperature as being near&nbsp;
600 kelvin. Previously scientists had taken what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:49.840
they thought they knew about the surface of Venus&nbsp;
and used that to try and work out a projected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:56.800
temperature but Sagan worked backwards, he took&nbsp;
this temperature measurement and tried to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:03.440
out how it came about. It was certainly higher&nbsp;
than the expected temperature of such a planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.480
which is quoted here as being&nbsp;
no more than 350 degrees kelvin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:16.480
and what Sagan says here is that it is clear that&nbsp;
a surface temperature of 600 kelvin demands a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:23.920
efficient greenhouse effect. So his calculations&nbsp;
found that essentially a runaway greenhouse effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.920
was able to explain this very high temperature.&nbsp;
This effect would occur when there are a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.920 --> 00:11:37.360
of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere trapping&nbsp;
radiation from the sun and heating up the planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:44.080
Carbon dioxide and water vapor are both&nbsp;
greenhouse gases and Sagan proposes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:51.360
it is water, excess water, in the atmosphere that&nbsp;
could explain the greenhouse effect on Venus but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:56.960
later missions to Venus actually found that&nbsp;
there was very little water in the atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:03.840
and instead it was almost entirely carbon dioxide.&nbsp;
The radiation balance of a planet is a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:11.600
delicate thing and it seems likely that Venus&nbsp;
wasn't always so hot and unwelcoming it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:18.000
have been much more liveable like Earth but it is&nbsp;
closer to the sun and hotter and drier than Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:25.280
and the carbon compounds might have evaporated&nbsp;
rather than remaining in the rocks leading to its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:30.960
greenhouse effect to essentially run away leading&nbsp;
to a vicious cycle where the planet just gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:36.880
hotter and hotter. Sagan discusses what might have&nbsp;
happened if the Earth had been placed in the orbit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:44.320
of Venus in its primitive times and essentially&nbsp;
its fate is much the same the oceans would boil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:49.120
the carbon dioxide abundance would go up and&nbsp;
Earth wouldn't be the home that we know it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:55.520
be. All of this research into what caused the&nbsp;
fate of Venus and thinking about what might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.520 --> 00:13:01.200
happen to Earth in different circumstances&nbsp;
probably led Carl Sagan to be quite worried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:06.800
about things like global warming and passionate&nbsp;
about trying to communicate what was going on.

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:10.480
Now no one has proposed that the trouble&nbsp;
with Venus is that there once was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:17.840
venusians who drove fuel inefficient cars but our&nbsp;
nearest neighbor nevertheless is a stark warning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.480
on the possible fate of an earth-like world.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:28.720
The last section of the thesis is this one here&nbsp;
which is actually an experimental project. Sagan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.720 --> 00:13:35.920
is again talking about how organic molecules are&nbsp;
produced when energy is supplied to an atmosphere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:44.000
of methane, ammonia ,water and hydrogen.&nbsp;
He uses spectroscopy to find the spectra of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:49.600
some of these known end products and lists&nbsp;
the features that he finds in this table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:58.160
What he then does is compares this laboratory&nbsp;
spectra to spectra taken with a telescope looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:05.040
at some of the jovian planets including Jupiter&nbsp;
and Saturn. From his observations he thinks that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:11.360
it's possible that there is hydrogen cyanide&nbsp;
present in the atmosphere of Jupiter however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:17.760
this was only a preliminary report and so he's&nbsp;
not able to really conclusively say these things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:22.720
but has set up this field of science&nbsp;
for some further investigation.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:27.920
And that is a look at Carl Sagan's&nbsp;
thesis which would set him up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.920 --> 00:14:32.880
a very successful career further&nbsp;
tackling all of these questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:37.520
thinking about the atmospheres of planets&nbsp;
and the likelihood of finding life there.

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.920
Thanks for watching and thanks as always to&nbsp;
my Patreon supporters who make these videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:50.080
possible. A special shout out to today's&nbsp;
Patron cats of the day, Minerva and Isis.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:57.948
I hope to see you next time :)

